==========================================================================================================================
L21 -- binary galaxies: does the kernel or the cosmic dark share govern the regime between galaxies and clusters?
==========================================================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 1 -- the two-body machinery.  Milgrom's exact deep-MOND two-body force, reused (not re-derived), and
          bridged to the carried kernel so both limits are exact.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Milgrom's two-body force in the form g02c grades it, F2/(M2 a_1) with a_1 = sqrt(G M1 a0)/d:
      1:1 ratio  0.55228   10:1 ratio  0.81378
      the repository's independent QUMOND field solve (g02c D1, both footings): 0.5579, 0.8205
  [PASS] C1 [CONTROL] the two-body machinery reproduces the programme's verified deep-MOND two-body force before it is used   (analytic 0.55229/0.81379 exact; vs g02c's field solve 1.01% and 0.82% (both < 1.1%))
  [PASS] C1b [CONTROL] the bridge's deep-MOND limit is Milgrom's own: v_rel -> 1.05099 (G m a0)^{1/4} for an equal pair   (recovered 1.05096 against the analytic 1.05099)

  Newtonian control -- the carried kernel is SATURATED, so at high acceleration it leaves a constant
  residual 0.6476 a0.  The relative acceleration must return to Newton as that residual becomes negligible:
    M_b(pair) [Msun]  r_M [kpc]    r/r_M    d [kpc]       g_N/a0   a_rel/a_Newton - 1
            2.00e+09       1.73     1.00      1.726            1            5.153e-01
            2.00e+09       1.73     0.10      0.173          100            6.476e-03
            2.00e+09       1.73     0.01      0.017        1e+04            6.476e-05
            1.60e+11      15.44     1.00     15.436            1            5.153e-01
            1.60e+11      15.44     0.10      1.544          100            6.476e-03
            1.60e+11      15.44     0.01      0.154        1e+04            6.476e-05
  [PASS] C2 [CONTROL] at small separation, where the acceleration is high, the prediction reduces to Newtonian   (at d = 0.01 r_M the carried kernel's saturated residual is 6.48e-05 of the Newtonian force (the residual is exactly 0.6476 a0/g_N and never vanishes -- that is the kernel's own structure))
  [PASS] C3 [CONTROL] the bridge is exact in BOTH limits for equal, 10:1 and dwarf pairs on both footings   (worst deviation 4.3e-06 at 1e5 r_M, falling by 100x for 100x in radius (the kernel's own O(sqrt(s)) approach to deep MOND, not a numerical residual); Newtonian arm exact to 6e-09)

  THE EXTERNAL FIELD, and the value used.  h81_h82_mw_external_fields.py computes it from the 2M++
  reconstruction (Carrick+2015) rather than assuming it: the Newtonian large-scale-structure field at the
  Local Group is g = 1.161e-12 m/s^2, i.e.
       canonical: e_N = 0.01240 a0;  QUMOND response nu_par = 5.004, nu_perp = 9.490, orientation-averaged nu_bar = 7.994
             alt: e_N = 0.01027 a0;  QUMOND response nu_par = 5.447, nu_perp = 10.376, orientation-averaged nu_bar = 8.733
  The pair scripts already in the repository used a nominal e_N = 0.02-0.03; the computed value is smaller,
  which pushes the isolated/EFE crossover OUT and is therefore the choice generous to the framework.
       canonical: for the median 2MRS pair (M_b = 1.6e11 Msun, r_M = 15.4 kpc) the isolated branch gives way
                  to the external-field branch at r = 144 kpc = 9.3 r_M
             alt: for the median 2MRS pair (M_b = 1.6e11 Msun, r_M = 14.1 kpc) the isolated branch gives way
                  to the external-field branch at r = 145 kpc = 10.3 r_M

