  the two curves CROSS at r_p ~ 70 kpc (g_N = 0.049 a0); below it the cosmic share
  predicts more, above it the framework's ISOLATED branch does.  Over an observable 30-1000 kpc the maximum
  divergence is 0.546 dex in velocity, at r_p = 1000 kpc.
  [FAIL] S0 [structure] the framework's honest external-field prediction is observationally DISTINCT from a cosmic-share halo at large separation   (beyond ~200 kpc BOTH are quasi-Newtonian with a constant boost -- the framework's orientation-averaged nu_bar(e_N) = 7.99 against the cosmic share's 1 + 5.43 = 6.43.  The predicted dispersions differ by only +11.5% in velocity (+24% in mass) at every separation and every mass, an EXACT degeneracy in shape.  Where the external field dominates, this programme's EFE and LambdaCDM's cosmic dark share are the same law with the same slope, separated only by that 12% in velocity -- below the stellar-M/L systematic)

  THE LADDER.  In one currency -- the dark-to-baryon mass ratio a Newtonian reading of the data requires --
  across the three regimes this programme has now measured:
                                regime    M_dark/M_bar required    framework residual after its kernel
          galaxy interiors (SPARC RAR) 0 (kernel suffices, 0.108 dex)                                      0
       isolated major pairs, this lane             30.9 +/- 1.6                     9.5 M_b (A = 1.80)
      X-COP clusters at 0.80 R500 (L7)            5.73 +/- 0.68      2.76 (alt) / 3.09 (canonical) M_b
  cosmic Omega_dm/Omega_b = 5.43
  [FAIL] S1 the dark-to-baryon ratio the pair data require is the SAME cosmic share the clusters require   (pairs need 30.9 +/- 1.6 WITHIN THE PAIR SEPARATION against the cosmic 5.43 -- 16.3 sigma away, a factor 5.7.  The cosmic share is a CLUSTER-scale statement, not a universal one; at pair separations galaxies are baryon-poor relative to cosmic and need several times more)
  [FAIL] S2 a cosmic-share halo -- the exact quantity L7 found clusters demand -- reproduces the pair kinematics   (cosmic share as a point mass A = 2.22 +/- 0.05 (22 sigma high), as an NFW halo A = 2.32 +/- 0.05 (25 sigma high).  Both UNDER-predict the observed dispersion; abundance-matched LambdaCDM, which puts 34x the baryons rather than 6.4x, lands at A = 0.97 +/- 0.02)
  [FAIL] S3 the framework's carried kernel reproduces the pair kinematics on its own   (carried (with the computed 2M++ external field) A = 2.31; the framework's BEST CASE, the isolated deep-MOND branch no external field can raise, A = 1.80 +/- 0.04 canonical / 1.73 alt, 20 sigma above 1, needing 8.3x the K-band baryonic mass)
  [FAIL] S4 the framework's kernel PLUS the cluster-scale cosmic share reproduces the pair kinematics   (A = 1.14 +/- 0.03, 5.0 sigma from 1 -- against 1.80 for the kernel alone and 2.22 for the cosmic share alone.  The combination is much closer to the data than either piece, and it is the only one of the three with no fitted parameter that lands within a factor 1.14)
  [PASS] S5 existing published data ALREADY discriminate the three curves at 3 sigma   (amplitudes: framework 20 sigma, cosmic share 22 sigma; separation slope -0.170 +/- 0.029 is 5.6 sigma from the framework's isolated branch and 10.6 sigma from the cosmic share.  The statistics are there; what is NOT settled is the isolation-depth systematic (see below))

  IF the pair deficit is a real dark component, WHERE must it sit?  The galaxy-side gate is the radial
  acceleration relation's 0.11 dex scatter: how much extra mass inside 10 kpc can the CARRIED kernel absorb
  before g_obs moves by more than that?  Computed here directly, not reused:
   M_b (each) [Msun]  RAR tolerance at 10 kpc   NFW cosmic share <10 kpc   over by
            2.00e+09                0.589 M_b                  1.895 M_b      3.22x
            8.00e+10                0.408 M_b                  0.337 M_b      0.82x
  (L1/L7 quote a tolerance of 0.25 M_b at 10 kpc from the galaxy side; the direct computation above gives
   0.41 M_b for an L* galaxy and 0.59 M_b for a dwarf -- the same order, and the dwarf end is the tight one.)
  [FAIL] S6 a cosmic-share dark component with an NFW shape is admissible inside galaxies on the RAR gate   (at L* an NFW cosmic share puts 0.34 M_b inside 10 kpc against a tolerance of 0.41 (0.82x, admissible); at the dwarf scale 1.90 against 0.59 (3.2x, EXCLUDED).  The galaxy-side objection therefore bites on SHAPE and on MASS SCALE, not on the amount: g04k/L1's cold infall delivers 0.92-1.45 M_b inside 10 kpc, 2.7x more concentrated than NFW at L*, and no dark component with a cuspy NFW inner slope survives at 2e9 Msun)

  SYSTEMATICS, restated because they bound everything above and were established by the prior lanes:
   1. ISOLATION DEPTH is the leading one.  2MRS at the sample's median distance sees only M_b > 3.6e10 Msun,
      so 'isolated' means 'no companion above ~23% of the pair's own mass'.  h48_h69 measured the amplitude
      falling from 1.99 in the far velocity shell to 1.51 in the near one, where the isolation reaches four
      times further down the luminosity function -- so every amplitude here is an UPPER limit on the real one.
   2. The ALFALFA dwarf pairs (h47, N = 138, velocities good to 4.4 km/s) give A(deep-MOND) = 1.79 +/- 0.20
      unrelatively isolated and 1.12 +/- 0.29 under the relative criterion -- 2.6 sigma from this sample's
      1.89 +/- 0.05.  The cross-scale tension is UNRESOLVED and this lane does not resolve it.
   3. Circular relative orbits are assumed.  Eccentric orbits at the same energy spend more time near
      apocentre at LOWER speed, so the assumption is generous to the framework.
   4. Upsilon_K = 0.6 and no gas in M_b.  The amplitude moves as Upsilon^{1/4} in the framework and
      Upsilon^{1/2} in the Newtonian laws, so closing the framework's gap needs Upsilon ~ 7, outside any
      stellar population; the SHAPE axis of PART 4 is immune to this.
   5. Pairs beyond ~500 kpc may not be bound; the interloper term removes chance projections, not
      physically associated unbound pairs.  Every amplitude above is dominated by r_p < 500 kpc.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE PRE-REGISTERABLE STATEMENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  For a pair of galaxies of baryonic masses M1, M2 at 3-D separation r, on a circular relative orbit, the
  framework's carried kernel predicts a line-of-sight velocity difference with dispersion
       sigma_los = sqrt(a_rel(r) r / 3),   a_rel = min[ G M_tot/r^2 + a0 Delta(G M_eff/(a0 r^2)),
                                                        nu_bar(e_N) G M_tot/r^2 ],
       M_eff = (2/3)^2 [(M1+M2)^{3/2} - M1^{3/2} - M2^{3/2}]^2 / mu^2,  mu = M1 M2/M_tot,
       Delta(s) = s/(exp(sqrt(s)) - 1) saturated at 0.6476 for s > 2.54, nu_bar(0.01240) = 7.994 / nu_bar(0.01027) = 8.733.
  For equal masses in the isolated branch this is the parameter-free
       sigma_los = (1.05099/sqrt(3)) (G m a0)^{1/4} = 0.60679 (G m a0)^{1/4}, INDEPENDENT OF SEPARATION,
       i.e. 107.7 km/s for two 8e10 Msun galaxies on the canonical footing, at ANY separation beyond ~30 kpc.
       i.e. 112.9 km/s for two 8e10 Msun galaxies on the alt footing, at ANY separation beyond ~30 kpc.
  PREDICTIONS THAT LOSE:
    P1 amplitude.  A = 1.  Measured on 1900 isolated 2MRS major pairs: A = 1.80 +/- 0.04 (canonical) / 1.73 (alt).  FALSIFIED at 20 sigma unless the isolation systematic carries it.
    P2 shape.  d log sigma / d log r_p = -0.010 on the isolated branch. Measured -0.170 +/- 0.029: 5.6 sigma.  This axis is immune to the stellar M/L and needs only N = 65 pairs.
    P3 mass.  d log sigma / d log M_b = +0.25 (deep MOND). The residual instead climbs with mass at 3.9 sigma across the sample.
    P4 the decisive future sample.  122 pairs with 10 km/s velocities separate the framework from a cosmic-share halo at 3 sigma on amplitude; DESI and 4MOST pair samples with HI or spectroscopic velocities reach that by two orders of magnitude.  What they must ALSO do is isolate against a catalogue two magnitudes deeper than 2MRS -- that, not statistics, is what is missing.

